            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/UseCases.fs",
                "line": 216,
                "quote": "commit ports (fun _ library ->\n                Catalog.addMember restored library"
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/UseCases.fs",
                "line": 219,
                "quote": "{ l with\n                        Fines = l.Fines @ fines\n                        Payments = l.Payments @ payments }"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The legality check, refusing a member who already exists, is left to Tier 2 (Catalog.addMember). Selecting the member's fines and payments also uses Tier 2 (Accounts.finesOf/paymentsOf). But the rest of the restore-member state change is written by hand in a Tier 3 use case: it appends fines and payments to the library record and decides that loans, holds and reminders stay behind. Tier 2 has no transition for this. This part of the domain change is decided in Tier 3 rather than requested from Tier 2. It does not contradict any Tier 2 rule, so the score is 1, not 0.",
